      "decision_index": 10,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The player is evoking Subtlety (which requires exiling a blue card from hand) and chose to exile Deceit instead of Force of Negation. Deceit is a powerful card that provides both bounce and hand disruption effects, and costs 6 mana making it harder to cast but very impactful. Force of Negation is also strong but is more situational (only counters noncreature spells, and its free cast only works on opponent's turn). However, in this deck that appears to be a graveyard-focused strategy with Overlord of the Balemurk and Persist, keeping Force of Negation is reasonable as protection against opposing interaction. But Deceit can be recurred via Overlord of the Balemurk's ability (returning a non-Avatar creature from graveyard to hand) if it ends up in the graveyard, whereas exiling it removes that option entirely. Force of Negation exiled is less of a loss since it can't be recurred anyway. The player should have exiled Force of Negation to preserve Deceit for potential recursion with Overlord of the Balemurk.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Deceit (a 6-mana creature with powerful ETB effects) to pay for Subtlety's evoke cost.",
      "better_line": "Exile Force of Negation instead, preserving Deceit which has synergy with Overlord of the Balemurk's recursion ability and is a more impactful card in this deck's game plan.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 6,
      "player": "GPT4om Valley",
      "decision_index": 10,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Swamp instead of Polluted Delta is slightly suboptimal. With Thoughtseize in hand wanting to cast it this turn (needing black mana), both lands work. However, Polluted Delta is generally better to play first because: 1) It can fetch a Swamp (or Island) later if needed, but playing it now and cracking it gives the same mana access while thinning the deck. 2) More importantly, keeping the fetchland uncracked preserves future flexibility, but playing Swamp now means the Polluted Delta will be played later when it could have been played turn 1 to fetch. The key consideration is that Psychic Frog wants cards in the graveyard for its exile-three ability, and a cracked fetchland contributes to graveyard count. Also, having a fetchland in play gives shuffle effects and flexibility. Playing Polluted Delta, fetching a Swamp (paying 1 life), and casting Thoughtseize would put a fetchland in the graveyard for Psychic Frog while preserving the basic Swamp for future land drops (basics can't be Wastelanded in formats that have it, though this is Modern). The life cost is minor (1 life for fetch + 2 for Thoughtseize = 3 total vs just 2 for Thoughtseize with Swamp), but graveyard building for Psychic Frog and deck thinning generally favor leading with the fetchland.",
      "action_taken": "Played Swamp as the turn 1 land drop",
